    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "the fluid looks pretty clean though. i did a drain and fill last week so it's unlikely to be old sludge. if the solenoids aren't the issue, could it be something internal like a failing seal or maybe a mechanical issue with the pump itself? what else should i be looking at?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "If the fluid looks clean, you're smart to rule out the obvious sludge, which brings up the possibility of a pump issue. A failing transmission pump can certainly cause inconsistent pressure delivery, leading to those erratic spikes you're seeing. It's also possible that a seal is leaking internally, which allows pressure to bleed off unpredictably during shifts, causing the control valves to react erratically. You might want to inspect the torque converter lock-up clutch circuit, as its operation is heavily dependent on stable hydraulic pressure.",
      "source": "target",
