    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "yeah i'll check the torque converter. it's just weird because it's a fairly new truck. sometimes the pressure is fine and then suddenly it just jumps way out of spec for no reason, like it's almost... random or something. is there any scenario where the electronics might be causing these kinds of jumpy readings instead of just mechanical wear?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Random, jumpy readings, even when the fluid looks clean, strongly suggests a control system hiccup, which brings the electronics into play. Even if the mechanical components are solid, a faulty sensor—like a pressure transducer—could be feeding the transmission control module (TCM) bad data, making the computer *think* the pressure is spiking when it isn't. Alternatively, a wiring harness issue or a failing solenoid driver within the TCM itself could be causing intermittent, erratic commands to the hydraulic valves. It's not always worn parts; sometimes it's just a bad signal telling the system to behave like a pile of loose gravel.",
      "source": "target",
